    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d999">

                <pb facs="0772" n="690" xml:id="pg690"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 853.00/169.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the American Chargé d’Affaires.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-03-03"><hi rendition="#italic">March 3,
                        1911</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 19.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department has received your No. 83 of
                    the 10th ultimo reporting the gist of a recent conversation between the minister
                    for foreign affairs of Portugal and you relative to the recognition of the
                    present Government of that country.</p>
                <p>Your legation was advised by cable on January 2nd of this Government’s attitude
                    at that time toward the <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> Government of
                    Portugal and on the 31st of the same month you were informed<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d999-f1"/> that this Government’s attitude was still one of
                    benevolent expectancy. This attitude toward the new Government of Portugal
                    remains precisely the same to-day.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d999-f1" n="1" xml:id="d999-f1-content">
                <p>This statement was later corrected in Instruction No. 31 (not printed), which
                    shows that the two cables were sent to the legation at Madrid.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
